INTRODUCTION

AmigaPython is a port of Python to classic AmigaOS.  This release is
Python 2.7.18 - the last Python 2 version from the Python Software
Foundation - continuing Irmen de Jong's AmigaPython line from Aminet.

This drawer is a complete, standalone release.  There is no installer
script.  Copy the drawer to your hard disk and run the Python program.

Language and library reference (upstream):
  https://docs.python.org/2.7/

amigazen project:
  https://github.com/amigazen/amigapython/
  http://www.amigazen.com/amigapython/

@EndNode


@Node Requirements "Requirements"
@Next "CopyAnywhere"
@Prev "Intro"
@Toc "Main"

REQUIREMENTS

 * Kickstart / AmigaOS 3.1 or higher 
 * 68020-class CPU or better (match the binary you received)
 * Enough RAM and stack for imports (raise stack if deep recursion fails)
 * Hard disk strongly recommended (library zip is large)

Optional:

 * bsdsocket.library (TCP stack such as Roadshow) for networking
 * usergroup.library for pwd / grp / crypt style APIs

I-Net225 is not supported.

@EndNode


@Node CopyAnywhere "Copy anywhere"
@Next "Shell"
@Prev "Requirements"
@Toc "Main"

COPY ANYWHERE

1. Unpack or copy this entire Python drawer to any volume.
2. Keep the layout intact - especially lib/ next to the Python binary.
3. Double-click Python, or run it from a Shell by full path.

No ASSIGN is required when the binary and lib/ stay together.

Optional convenience in S:User-Startup:

  ASSIGN Python: Work:Python
  PATH Python: ADD

(Replace Work:Python with your real path.)

Then you can type "Python" from any Shell and use Default Tool
Python:Python on project icons.

@EndNode


@Node Shell "Shell usage"
@Next "Workbench"
@Prev "CopyAnywhere"
@Toc "Main"

SHELL USAGE

  Python
      Interactive interpreter.

  Python script.py
      Run a script.  Arguments after the script go to sys.argv.

  Python -c "print 'hello'"
      Run a one-liner.

  Python -?
      Short usage / options hint.

  Python -c "import test.pystone; test.pystone.main()"
      Simple speed benchmark.

Environment variables (optional):

  PYTHONHOME    Override prefix
  PYTHONPATH    Semicolon-separated extra module directories
  PYTHONCASEOK  Relax import filename case matching

Help text for this command: Python.help (also "Help Python" if Help:
can see it).

@EndNode


@Node Workbench "Workbench usage"
@Next "Libraries"
@Prev "Shell"
@Toc "Main"

WORKBENCH USAGE

 * Double-click the Python icon for a CON: console window.
 * Close with the window close gadget or Ctrl+C.
 * Project icons: set Default Tool to this Python binary, or to
   Python:Python if you created the ASSIGN.  The project file is
   passed as the script to run.
 * Tooltypes on the tool icon can add arguments.  Special names:
     PYTHONSCRIPT=path
     PYSCRIPTARG=arg
 * Do not unlock Workbench's own locks; leave startup locks alone.

If Python cannot find lib/python27.zip beside the binary and Python:
is not assigned, you get an error requester explaining what to fix.

@EndNode


@Node Libraries "Libraries"
@Next "AmigaModules"
@Prev "Workbench"
@Toc "Main"

LIBRARIES

Release layout (next to the Python binary):

  lib/python27.zip     Standard library (zipimport)
  lib/lib-dynload/     Built-in extension modules (.so-style)
  lib/site-python/     Amiga-only pure-Python helpers

getpath looks next to the executable first, then falls back to an
optional Python: assign.  Keep this drawer intact when copying.

Put your own Amiga-only modules in lib/site-python (or a directory on
PYTHONPATH).  Do not patch portable stdlib modules lightly.

@EndNode


@Node AmigaModules "Amiga modules"
@Next "Networking"
@Prev "Libraries"
@Toc "Main"

AMIGA MODULES

The builtin "amiga" module is the Amiga stand-in for Unix "posix" and
is used by "os".  Extra helpers (Dos, ASL, catalogs, icons, path
converters) are documented in Help/Amiga/*.rst.

Site-python shims (examples):

  arexx, asl, catalog, icon, dos, amigavars, intuition, ...

Import portable code via "os" / "os.path".  Use "import amiga" only
when you need Amiga-specific APIs.

See Help/Amiga/readme for the reST document index.

@EndNode


@Node Networking "Networking"
@Next "Documentation"
@Prev "AmigaModules"
@Toc "Main"

NETWORKING

Socket modules need bsdsocket.library from a modern TCP stack
(Roadshow and similar).  Start the stack before running network demos
or socket tests.

I-Net225 is not supported.

pwd / grp / crypt may need usergroup.library.

Demo scripts: Demo/ (see Demo/readme).  Offline samples under
Demo/scripts/ and Demo/amiga/; TCP demos under Demo/sockets/ and
Demo/chatserver.py.

@EndNode


